.TH METARM 1 2026-11-20 "metarm 0.2.0"

.SH NAME
metarm \- remove metadata from files

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B metarm
[\fB\-rRfaiv\fR]
.I file ...

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B metarm
strips metadata (EXIF, GPS location, XMP, IPTC, comments, text chunks,
timestamps) from each given
.IR file ,
rewriting it in place. It is invoked like
.BR rm (1):
directories require
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-f
is accepted so that muscle memory such as
.B metarm \-rf dir
works.
.PP
By default a handful of tags that only affect how the image is displayed are
kept, so photos still appear the right way up: orientation, resolution
(X/Y resolution and unit) and colour space. They are copied into a newly
built EXIF block, never passed through, so nothing else from the original
EXIF survives. Use
.B \-a
to remove those as well.
.PP
Image data is never re-encoded. Only the metadata segments are dropped;
colour information needed to display the image correctly (ICC profiles,
gamma, transparency) is kept. Files are replaced atomically and keep their
permissions.
.PP
Formats are detected by content, not by file extension. Supported formats:
JPEG, PNG (including APNG), WebP.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R ", " \-\-recursive
process directories recursively. Unsupported files inside directories are
skipped silently, and symbolic links are not followed.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
has no effect; accepted for compatibility with
.BR rm (1).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
remove all metadata, including the orientation, resolution and colour-space
tags kept by default.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-info
list the metadata in each file without changing it. Each line shows whether
stripping would
.B keep
or
.B strip
the item, taking
.B \-a
into account. Unlike
.BR rm (1),
.B \-i
does not mean interactive.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
print each file as it is processed. With
.BR \-i ,
show long values in full instead of shortening them.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
display help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
print version information.

.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every named file was processed successfully, 1 otherwise. A file named
directly on the command line that is not a supported format is an error.

.SH NOTES
With
.BR \-a ,
photos that relied on the orientation tag may display rotated.
.PP
ICC colour profiles are kept even with
.BR \-a ,
since removing them changes how colours are displayed.
.PP
Only metadata embedded in the file is removed. The file name, timestamps and
extended attributes are left unchanged, and nothing can be done about
identifying features in the pixels themselves.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B metarm photo.jpg
strip one file
.TP
.B metarm \-rf ~/Pictures/upload
strip every supported image under a directory
.TP
.B metarm \-a photo.jpg
strip everything, including orientation
.TP
.B metarm \-ri ~/Pictures
list the metadata of every image under a directory

.SH AUTHOR
Kris Huber <kris@krishuber.xyz>

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrm\fR(1)
